+# Decision: Self-Hosted WebSocket Delivery of Committed Changes
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard boards are watched by up to 800 staff browsers at once, and a committed edit has to reach connected browsers promptly: the project goal allows 15 seconds under normal connectivity. Editors commit changes over HTTPS, and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision per event. The question this decision settles is how committed changes reach browsers — by polling an HTTPS endpoint, by pushing over WebSocket connections the project operates, or by publishing to a hosted realtime service.
+
+The choice is not contained in one component. Each route changes what the change store must support, what the delivery node owns, and what the browser client must implement, so it sits at the root of the design tree and sizes three downstream nodes: change-store, delivery, and browser-sync.
+
+Two constraints inherited from the high-level design frame the options. "Vendor payments" is an explicit non-goal, and the tenet "Own the realtime path" leans the project toward infrastructure it operates rather than a managed realtime vendor. Event updates carry stall IDs and operational statuses rather than customer records, so no option is excluded on data-handling grounds.
+
+The stakes are asymmetric. A polling choice leaves the board visibly stale during opening-time bursts and multiplies store reads across the browser fleet. A hosted choice makes the board's update path depend on a third party's pricing and availability, against the project's stated boundaries.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates** — binary criteria. An option that fails a gate is eliminated.
+
+- **No recurring third-party realtime fees.** Inherited from the high-level design's non-goal on vendor payments. A route whose cost includes per-connection or per-message billing from a realtime provider is eliminated.
+- **The update path is one the project operates.** Inherited from the tenet "Own the realtime path". A route that requires a third-party realtime service to deliver committed changes is eliminated.
+
+**Weighted criteria** — graded considerations.
+
+- **Vendor independence (major).** Importance follows from the tenet "Own the realtime path": the update path should not be shaped by a third-party realtime vendor. Distinguishes routes the project operates from a hosted service.
+- **Latency of committed changes (major).** Committed changes should reach connected browsers promptly. The project goal permits 15 seconds, and the board's value during opening-time bursts depends on updates arriving without a standing delay.
+- **Operating cost trajectory (moderate).** The marginal cost of the delivery path as the browser fleet and the edit rate grow, including infrastructure and any third-party fees.
+- **Operational burden absorbed by the project (moderate).** The ongoing work the project owns: connection capacity, session lifecycle, failure handling, and any provider relationship.
+- **Read amplification against the change store (moderate).** How many store reads one committed revision costs across the browser fleet.
+- **Failure blast radius of the delivery tier (moderate).** What happens to connected browsers when the delivery tier itself fails, and whether the board stays usable.
+- **Recovery of a long-disconnected browser (minor).** How a browser disconnected for up to 20 minutes returns to the latest board.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers poll a read endpoint on a fixed five-second interval and replace their view when the returned revision is newer. The server answers each poll with the board's current state or with the revisions the browser has not seen. Delivery stays request/response: there is no persistent connection and no server-side session state. The change store needs to answer cursor-style reads, and the browser runs a polling loop that backs off on failure.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- *No recurring third-party realtime fees:* passes.
+- *The update path is one the project operates:* passes.
+- *Vendor independence:* strong fit — no third-party service appears in the path.
+- *Latency of committed changes:* weak fit — a change becomes visible only at the next poll, up to five seconds after commit, and every browser learns of it through its own independent poll.
+- *Operating cost trajectory:* strong fit — marginal cost is periodic reads and no per-connection fees.
+- *Operational burden absorbed by the project:* strong fit — no session lifecycle, no connection capacity to size; failures are per request.
+- *Read amplification against the change store:* weak fit — 800 browsers polling every five seconds produce roughly 160 reads per second per event, most of them for unchanged state.
+- *Failure blast radius of the delivery tier:* strong fit — the tier holds no state, so a server restart interrupts at most one poll cycle.
+- *Recovery of a long-disconnected browser:* partial fit — catch-up is a natural cursor read, but the browser still waits for the next poll.
+
+**Summary.** The simplest and cheapest route to operate, at the cost of repeated reads and a standing delay on every change.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSocket delivery
+
+**Description.** The server maintains one WebSocket connection per connected browser. Editors still commit over HTTPS; the server publishes each committed revision to the sockets subscribed to that event, so browsers see the change as it is committed. The change store keeps revisions in a replayable order so a browser that missed updates can ask for exactly what it missed; the browser reconnects on its own after a dropped connection and then catches up over HTTPS. The project sizes connection capacity and owns session lifecycle, reconnection, and catch-up on both ends.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- *No recurring third-party realtime fees:* passes.
+- *The update path is one the project operates:* passes.
+- *Vendor independence:* strong fit — the path is infrastructure the project operates.
+- *Latency of committed changes:* strong fit — a committed revision is pushed as it is committed, comfortably inside the 15-second goal and normally subsecond.
+- *Operating cost trajectory:* strong fit — cost is the project's own compute and network; no per-connection or per-message fee.
+- *Operational burden absorbed by the project:* partial fit — connection capacity for 800 browsers, session lifecycle, reconnection, and catch-up are the project's to build and run.
+- *Read amplification against the change store:* strong fit — one store read per revision and one push per subscribed browser, rather than one read per browser per interval.
+- *Failure blast radius of the delivery tier:* partial fit — a delivery-tier restart drops every socket at once and the fleet reconnects in a wave, though every board stays correct once catch-up runs.
+- *Recovery of a long-disconnected browser:* strong fit — replayable revisions let a returning browser fetch precisely what it missed.
+
+**Summary.** The fastest updates and the least read amplification, at the cost of owning connections, reconnection, and catch-up.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** The server publishes committed changes to a third-party realtime service, and browsers subscribe to the service's channels. The service holds the connections, fans out messages, and handles client reconnection for consumers that use its client library. The server keeps a publication path whose delivered messages are reconciled against the authoritative revision stream, because a provider's delivery guarantee does not replace the project's own record of what was committed.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- *No recurring third-party realtime fees:* eliminated — the service bills per connection or per message once the browser fleet is at scale.
+- *The update path is one the project operates:* eliminated — the update path depends on the provider's channels, pricing, and availability.
+- *Vendor independence:* weak fit — a third party governs the shape and cost of the update path.
+- *Latency of committed changes:* strong fit — provider push is prompt.
+- *Operating cost trajectory:* weak fit — recurring fees scale with connections and messages.
+- *Operational burden absorbed by the project:* strong fit — the provider holds connections, reconnection, and fan-out.
+- *Read amplification against the change store:* strong fit — the server publishes once per revision regardless of browser count.
+- *Failure blast radius of the delivery tier:* partial fit — a provider outage silences updates fleet-wide; recovery still belongs to the project.
+- *Recovery of a long-disconnected browser:* partial fit — provider replay assists catch-up, but reconciliation against the authoritative revisions remains the project's work.
+
+**Summary.** The least connection work and prompt updates, at the price of recurring fees and provider dependence.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSocket delivery is chosen. The hosted realtime service fails both gates outright: the project holds no vendor payments as a non-goal, and the tenet "Own the realtime path" places the update path on infrastructure the project operates rather than on a provider's channels. Eliminating it on gates rather than on weighted criteria is deliberate — the project has already drawn that boundary, so the comparison among remaining routes is about cost and mechanics, not about whether a vendor belongs in the path.
+
+Among the routes the project operates, WebSockets win over polling on two major criteria. On latency, a committed revision is pushed as it is committed, while polling makes every change wait for the next five-second tick and makes each browser learn of the change on its own. On read amplification, one push per revision replaces roughly 160 polling reads per second per event. Polling matches or beats WebSockets on operating cost and on operational burden, and it remains the natural fallback if the socket path's operational cost proves heavier than expected.
+
+The chosen path's weaker fits are accepted knowingly. The project owns connection capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers, session lifecycle, reconnection, and catch-up — the work polling would have avoided. A delivery-tier restart drops every socket at once and the fleet reconnects in a wave; catch-up over HTTPS keeps every board correct, but running that recovery is the project's job. This tradeoff is the operational expression of the tenet "Own the realtime path", which treats owned infrastructure as worth its maintenance cost when it keeps the board's update path free of vendor fees and vendor coupling.
+
+**Implications for downstream nodes.**
+
+- Change store must keep revisions in a replayable order and support catch-up reads for a browser returning after up to 20 minutes.
+- Delivery must own session lifecycle, event subscriptions, and the publication of committed revisions to subscribed sessions, including the behavior when publication fails.
+- Browser sync must own reconnect and catch-up, and must make connectivity failure visible rather than letting a stale board look current.
+
+**Forecloses.** Recurring per-connection or per-message realtime fees, and any dependence on a provider's channel model, pricing, or availability.
+
+**Leaves open.** Whether the socket tier runs on infrastructure the project already operates or on separately sized capacity.
